# CLAUDE.md

This is the jarvOS Claude Code baseline. It is materialized into
`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` by the jarvOS runtime adapter and therefore loads at the
start of **every** Claude Code session on this user account, not just sessions
inside a jarvOS clone.

### Scope

- The sections below apply when the current work touches a jarvOS-managed
  workspace (a jarvOS clone, the public `levineam/jarvOS` repo, or a workspace
  configured with jarvOS modules and the `jarvos` MCP server).
- Outside that context, treat the **jarvOS Release Targeting** and **jarvOS
  Upstream Evaluation** sections as informational. Defer to the workspace's
  own `CLAUDE.md`, conventions, and review process before applying jarvOS
  routing rules.
- Identity, governance, and hydration notes describe the environment Claude
  Code is running in; they do not assert anything about the active workspace.

## Identity

- Runtime: Claude Code (CLI). Sibling runtimes used elsewhere by jarvOS:
  OpenClaw, Hermes, Codex.
- When working in a jarvOS workspace: persona and identity references live in
  the jarvOS core files (`core/AGENTS.md`, `core/SOUL.md`, `core/IDENTITY.md`
  in the jarvOS repo).

## Governance

jarvOS governance lives in markdown. Read the source files before acting on
non-trivial work; do not rely on this summary alone.

- **AGENTS.md** (jarvOS core) — portable behavioral rules
- **SOUL.md** / **IDENTITY.md** (jarvOS core) — persona and tone
- Workspace-local `CRITICAL-RULES.md`, `CONSTITUTION.md`, `skills/RESOLVER.md`
  if present — they override anything below when they conflict

### Runtime applicability of CRITICAL-RULES

If the workspace ships a `CRITICAL-RULES.md`, apply every rule unless it is
explicitly OpenClaw-runtime-specific. The following CRs are OpenClaw-only and
do not apply to Claude Code:

| CR | Why OpenClaw-only |
|---|---|
| Sessions-spawn model parameter rules | Claude Code uses the `Agent` tool (`subagent_type`), not `sessions_spawn`. |
| Default subagent model rules (gpt-5.x, GLM) | Claude Code runs Anthropic models. Map equivalents: Opus for high-judgment, Sonnet for standard, Haiku for fast. |
| ACP-first / PTY-fallback coding rules | Claude Code has its own coding loop; ACP routing is not exposed here. |
| Agent-specific Paperclip routing rules (e.g., Michael) | Not applicable when Charlie/Claude Code is the executor. |

All other rules apply directly. In particular: external-message approval,
plan-first, fix-immediately, tiered auto-merge for safety-rail files,
retry-with-context for subagents, Paperclip blocker routing, QMD-first (mapped
to `jarvos_recall` here), read-before-acting under ambiguity, rule-wiring
auto-trigger, and enforcement assessment for HARD rules.

## jarvOS Release Targeting

jarvOS versions itself with release-please (`release-please-config.json`
at the repo root): the version number is derived automatically from
conventional-commit types on merged commits, not chosen by hand. `fix:`
yields a patch bump, `feat:` a minor bump. Picking the right commit type
is the entire versioning decision — there is no "target version" to
choose, and no version number belongs in a branch name, PR title, or
this file. Release *placement* — which release lane a piece of work
belongs to — is a separate question owned by the steward system in
`modules/jarvos-coding` (`triageCodingWork`,
`releaseFitFromPaperclipReleaseIntake`), which classifies work into a
product lane, release lane, ready-execution lane, or manual triage.

### What this triggers

- **Commit type** — `fix:` for bug fixes, doc corrections, install-flow
  fixes, smoke-test gaps; `feat:` for new capabilities, new MCP tools,
  new runtime adapters, new skills, workflow improvements.
- **Branch naming and PR titles** — mirror the commit type, e.g.
  `fix/SUP-XXXX/...` / `fix(SUP-XXXX): …` or `feat/SUP-XXXX/...` /
  `feat(SUP-XXXX): …`.
- **CHANGELOG.md** — release-please maintains this file; do not hand-edit
  version headers.
- **Scope discipline** — reject scope creep that crosses release lane
  boundaries, even though lanes are no longer numbered by hand.

### Ambiguity rule

If the right commit type or release lane is unclear: ask the user or
consult the `jarvos-coding` release intake, do not guess.

## jarvOS Upstream Evaluation (Proactive)

When working anywhere in a workspace that contains a jarvOS clone or sits
adjacent to one, evaluate each meaningful change for upstream candidacy. Do
not wait to be asked.

### When to evaluate

Trigger this check whenever the work in question:

- Adds or modifies a script, skill, MCP tool, or runtime hook
- Establishes a new pattern (file convention, workflow gate, contract)
- Fixes a bug in code that has a public equivalent in the jarvOS repo
- Creates documentation that explains a generalizable concept

Skip this check for: content edits (notes, journal, ontology data), personal
configuration, one-off scripts, anything obviously user-specific.

### Evaluation criteria, in order

1. **Generalizable?** Would this help a stranger cloning jarvOS, or does it
   only make sense for the current user's setup?
2. **Code, not content?** Per `PUBLIC_BASELINE.md`, code is public and content
   is private. Hardcoded paths, personal IDs, vault content, and secrets
   disqualify a candidate until refactored.
3. **Commit type?** Apply the targeting rules above — `fix:` vs `feat:`,
   and check placement with `jarvos-coding` release intake if unclear.
4. **Risk?** Identity, privacy, external-send, and marketing surfaces require
   extra scrutiny under the tiered auto-merge rule.

### How to surface candidates

When a candidate is found, raise it inline using this format:

> **Upstream candidate:** [one-line description]
> **Commit type:** fix / feat — [reason]
> **What it would take:** [refactor needs, e.g., extract hardcoded path, add
> config var, write template]
> **Recommend:** [ship now / queue for later / pass]

Do not open PRs, branches, or Paperclip issues unilaterally — surface the
candidate and let the user decide.

### What not to do

- Do not restructure private content to make it upstreamable without asking.
- Do not lift code that depends on private modules (e.g., personal-ontology
  data) without first identifying the abstraction needed.
- Do not promote every change. Most workspace work is local-only by design.

## Working Context Hydration

Every Claude Code session in this workspace starts with a hydrated Working
Context Packet, injected by the jarvOS `SessionStart` hook (configured in
`~/.claude/settings.json`). It includes current Paperclip work, today's
journal, linked notes, the jarvOS ontology context packet, and a Hydration
Report. The same hook re-fires after compact and resume with a smaller packet
so the session can re-orient without a full re-brief.

### MCP tools (registered as `jarvos`)

- `jarvos_current_work` — compact Paperclip current-work summary
- `jarvos_recall` — GBrain/graph recall bundle (use in place of QMD; same brain)
- `jarvos_create_note` — Obsidian note creation with today's journal wikilink
- `jarvos_startup_brief` — bounded startup context
- `jarvos_hydrate` — bounded working-context packet (also used for Claude
  Desktop manual hydration via the `boot jarvOS` prompt)

## Ontology Context Rule

Claude Code should load ontology through `jarvos_hydrate` or the shared
`@jarvos/ontology` provider. The ontology packet is hierarchy-of-meaning
context, not task state and not raw memory. Claude Code must not directly
mutate ontology source files or rewrite `ONTOLOGY.md`; secondbrain evidence can
only create source-backed ontology candidates or inquiry items for review.

## Secondbrain Capture Rule

For intentional capture requests such as `note:`, `make a note`, `idea:`, or
`save this`, Claude Code should call the jarvOS universal capture contract
instead of raw-writing Markdown. Use source `claude-code`.

Use the trusted absolute path materialized by jarvOS setup (never resolve the
capture script relative to the current workspace):

```bash
node {{JARVOS_CAPTURE_SCRIPT}}
```

Successful note captures must write under the configured `Notes/` directory,
link exactly once from `Journal/YYYY-MM-DD.md`, preserve source-backed
provenance, and leave QMD/search freshness as `pending-refresh`. Do not create
guessed daily journal files under `Notes/`.

## jarvOS Tool Doctrine

- `jarvos_recall` — for people, decisions, prior reasoning, and history.
  Trigger: "why did we choose X", "what did we decide about Y", "who is Z".
  Not for code or file contents; use grep/read for those. It searches a
  curated knowledge base, not the repo.
- `jarvos_session_thread_write` — write a checkpoint when a task switches,
  when a decision is made, or before a long session compact. A hook can
  record what changed mechanically; only the agent can record why. This
  is the surface that carries judgment.
- `jarvos_create_note` — when a session produces a durable conclusion
  someone would want months later. Not for routine progress.
- `jarvos_current_work` / `jarvos_projects_context` — consult at the start
  of substantive work to orient on what is already in flight, before
  proposing new work.
- `jarvos_todo_*` — claim an item before starting work another agent might
  also pick up. Claiming is what stops two harnesses doing the same job.

If a tool returns unavailable or stale, say so plainly rather than silently
working around it.

## Local Extensions

This file is generated from `runtimes/claude/templates/CLAUDE.md.template` in
the jarvOS repo, then materialized into `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. To add
workspace-specific behavior (pointers to local `CRITICAL-RULES.md`,
`RESOLVER.md`, `CONSTITUTION.md`, project-specific routing notes, etc.), add
them below this line. The setup script does not overwrite local edits
without first creating a backup.

